29.5  Copying files over SSH: scp, sftp and rsync

TL;DR
  - Everything that copies over SSH uses the same keys and the same
    ~/.ssh/config names: if ssh db works, scp file db: works.
  - scp for a quick copy, rsync for folders and anything big (it resumes and
    only sends differences), sshfs to mount a remote folder.
  - A path without a leading / after host: starts in your home folder there.

      scp report.pdf db:/tmp/
      scp -r db:logs/ ./logs-db/
      rsync -avP project/ db:project/      # resumable, shows progress

Pitfalls
  - rsync src/ dst and rsync src dst differ: the trailing slash copies the
    folder's contents, not the folder (28.7).
  - Interrupted scp starts over; rsync -P picks up where it stopped.
  - Remote paths with spaces need quoting twice with old scp: once for your
    shell, once for the remote one. Recent OpenSSH no longer needs it.
  - sshfs over a slow link makes every ls slow: copy what you work on.

Deep dive

  ▾ What changed in scp?

    The old scp protocol ran a shell command on the server and trusted its
    output, which caused security bugs and odd quoting. Since OpenSSH 9.0, scp
    speaks the SFTP protocol underneath, with the same command line. scp -O
    brings back the old protocol for servers without SFTP.

  ▾ Why is rsync faster on the second run?

    It compares sizes and dates, and for changed files, checksums of blocks,
    then sends only the blocks that differ. Copying a 10 GiB folder again after
    a few edits sends a few megabytes.

Related
  Commands  scp, sftp, rsync, sshfs
  See also  atfq 28.7 rsync
  See also  atfq 29.2 ~/.ssh/config, jump hosts and host keys
